  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 331 AM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nOvernight, a cluster of thunderstorms has persisted generally south \nof US-36, following along a pronounced LLJ and synoptic-scale \ndiffluence in the mid-upper levels of the atmosphere. Amidst ~40 kts \nof effective bulk shear and 1000-2000 J/kg of MUCAPE persisting \nafter sunset, these storms have remained robust enough for damaging \nwinds and hail to impact portions of the CWA. Through the remainder \nof the overnight , the severe threat will begin to diminish with \ndecreasing CAPE and effective shear as the LLJ shifts off towards \nthe south and east. However, another caveat of tonight's activity \nhas been very efficient rainfall rates out of thunderstorms, with \nenhanced moisture convergence along a stationary boundary draped \nacross northwestern Missouri and PWATs around 2-2.5 inches. Although \nactivity has become more spaced out through the night, thinking for \nthe remainder of the overnight period is that, while the severe \nthreat will continue diminishing, the hydrological threat will \ncontinue through tomorrow morning with any convection that fires \nalong the LLJ and broad shortwave trough in the low/mid-levels.\n\nThrough the late morning, the orientation of the LLJ will shift more \nsouthwesterly/northeasterly as it progresses into central MO, with \nresidual precipitation following along its nose. High pressure \nsituated over the northern Plains building into northern MO may lead \nto some patchy fog through the morning, but confidence is not high \nenough for widespread dense fog development under residual cloud \ncover from tonight's activity. Dry air dredging in behind the \nretreating shortwave will help dry out the forecast into tomorrow \nfor parts of the area, with temperatures residing in the 80s. \nHowever, south of I-70, a continued severe threat exists with warm \nadvection and higher dewpoints enhancing instability during the day. \nThe greatest hazards with this severe threat would be heavy rainfall \nand strong winds, with varied solutions on exact coverage/timing of \nthunderstorms during the day. The overall synoptic pattern looks to \nremain similar through the end of the week, with the forecast area \nresiding along the periphery of an upper-level ridge situated over \nthe southwestern CONUS and an upper-level trough over the Hudson \nBay. Under this setup, additional disturbances in the flow will lead \nto continued PoPs through Friday evening. Going into the weekend, \nthe upper-level ridge over the southwestern CONUS looks to build \nmore over the forecast area, pushing the upper-level disturbances \nand subsequent PoPs more towards the north and prevailing low-level \nwinds out of the south. With this adjustment, temperatures will \nclimb into the 90s by early next week, with coincident rising \ndewpoints helping to bring apparent temperatures into the 90s and \neven the triple digits.",
